<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=stepwell">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Building and engineering</p><h1 class="enc-title">Stepwell</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as vav, baori, baoli</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A stepwell, or vav, is a well, cistern or pond reached by a long corridor of steps down to the water, developed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, where the earliest archaeological evidence is at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dholavira">Dholavira</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a>'s inscriptions describe stepwells dug along major roads as an established practice of earlier kings, and the first rock-cut examples date from 200–400 CE. Built in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> around 600 CE, they spread north to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a>, and their construction peaked under Muslim rule from the 11th to the 16th century. Lined with unmortared stone, they gave year-round access to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/groundwater-depletion">groundwater</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/stepwell.jpg" alt="Stepwell" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bernard Gagnon · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rani_ki_vav_02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>A stepwell has two parts: a vertical shaft from which water is drawn, and the inclined passages, chambers and steps that give access to it. The galleries and chambers around the shaft were often carved profusely and became cool, quiet retreats in the hot summers. Some stepwells have several storeys, and water could be raised to the first or second floor by a Persian wheel pulled by a bull.</p><p>The names vary by region. In Hindi-speaking areas they include baoli, baori and bavadi, in Gujarati and Marwari vav or vavri, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a> kalyani or pushkarani. A related form, the stepped pond, usually stands beside a temple, is open to the sun and is rectangular, whereas a stepwell is dark, more linear and more isolated.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>A stepwell may have first appeared in periods of drought to secure access to water. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dholavira">Dholavira</a> has tanks with flights of steps, and the great bath at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohenjo-daro">Mohenjo-daro</a> has steps on opposite sides. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a>'s Pillar Edict No 7 says that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">wells</a> were dug and flights of steps built every eight kos along roads, and that this was an old practice of former kings.</p><p>One of the earliest bath-like ponds reached by steps lies in the Uperkot caves at Junagadh, dated to the 4th century, and the stepwells at Dhank in Rajkot district are dated to 550-625 CE. The Mata Bhavani stepwell in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, built in the 11th century, is among the earliest surviving examples and has elaborately ornamented columns, brackets and beams.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Stepwells gave water in a land where fresh water is abundant only in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monsoon">monsoon</a>. Because they were built without mortar, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/groundwater-depletion">groundwater</a> filtered through the stone, and at depth the water was less exposed to sun and heat. Most surviving stepwells served for bathing, washing, farming and watering animals, and as places for social gatherings and religious ceremonies. Women, who collected the water, prayed at them and offered gifts to the goddess of the well. Stepwells were also thought to spread disease, since the water attracted insects and animals.</p><p>The Mughal emperors encouraged the building of stepwells, while British authorities in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-raj">Raj</a> found their hygiene poor and installed pipes and pumps. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babur">Babur</a>'s garden at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agra">Agra</a>, the Aram Bagh, is said to have drawn on stepwells for its reflecting pool. The Stepwell Atlas, begun in 2016, mapped over 2,800 stepwells in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, and some have been restored to their original purpose as water shortages have grown.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Stepwell” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Bijapur itself grew into a large walled city with markets, mosques, palaces and an elaborate water supply of tanks, wells and channels, among them the great <b>stepwell</b> known as the Taj Bawdi.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bijapur-sultanate">Bijapur Sultanate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Its monuments include the Jami Masjid of 1423, Teen Darwaza, Bhadra Fort and the Dada Harir <b>Stepwell</b>, and Champaner, the 16th-century capital, survives as an archaeological park.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gujarat-sultanate">Gujarat Sultanate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Bhima I, Mahmud of Ghazni sacked the Somnath temple in 1025, but the kingdom recovered quickly: the Modhera Sun temple and the Rani ki Vav <b>stepwell</b>, built by Bhima's queen Udayamati, date from his reign, and his minister Vimala built the first Dilwara temple at Mount Abu.</blockquote><a href="/state/chaulukya-dynasty">Chaulukya dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>2650–1450 BCE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Dholavira, India</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd><a href="/culture/indus-valley-civilisation">Indus Valley Civilisation</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>The earliest traces are at Dholavira, a city of the Indus Valley Civilisation; Ashoka’s inscriptions describe stepwells along the roads, and the first rock-cut ones date from 200–400 CE.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/irrigation">Irrigation<span>c. 6000 BCE</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=stepwell">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
